  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThis Afternoon Through Sunday: Showers and Thunderstorms Return\n\nAnother beautiful weather day will occur this afternoon across the \nDriftless region! Very similar to the last few days, a light breeze \nwill be in the air and temperatures will rise into the upper 70s to \nlow 80s for highs this afternoon. Today would be a great day to get \noutdoors and enjoy the nice weather before the potential for rain \nreturns as well as warmer temperatures.\n\nSpeaking of the rain potential, northerly flow aloft today begins to \nbecome more northwest to west this evening into Saturday morning. \nThis change in the upper level flow opens the door to shortwave \ntroughs to ride the top of the ridge through the weekend. Strong mid \nlevel theta-e advection will occur out ahead of the first \ndisturbance that is progged to move over the Upper Midwest beginning \nSaturday morning. Widespread showers should form as a result and the \nsystem should move from west to east during Saturday morning into \nthe afternoon. There may be just enough instability present for a \nfew storms to form, but they will be few and far between. The 28/12z \nHREF mean PWAT values are near the climatological max based on \nsounding data from KMPX, so the rain showers may be efficient rain \nproducers, especially with any stronger cores/storms that may form. \nEven though there could be heavy rain at times, flooding is not \nexpected as the system will be fairly quick moving.\n\nThe potential for rainfall on Sunday is more uncertain as there are \ntiming and mesoscale differences in the forecast guidance. Right now \nthere are off and on chances throughout the day given the \nuncertainty. Some forecast models do depict the potential for some \nstorms to become strong to severe on Sunday afternoon into the \nevening, but confidence in that scenario panning out are very, \nvery low right now. A majority of guidance depicts strong WAA at\nH850 which would lead to strong capping, but there are a few \nmodels hinting that the WAA may be overdone. If the severe storm\nthreat were to pan out, damaging wind gusts and large hail \nwould be the primary hazards. Temperatures on Sunday could be \nquite warm in response to the increased low level temperatures \nwith forecast highs in the mid to upper 80s.\n\nNext Week: Daily Rain Chances with Warm Conditions\n\nThere is fairly good consistency to the expected upper level pattern \nfor next week, with the center of high pressure sitting over the \nsoutheast and troughing across the northwestern CONUS. The northern \nextent of the ridge is the main area of uncertainty right now and \nwill play a large factor in the weather for next week. The GFS/GEFS \nare currently an outlier in the ensemble model suite and they are \nindicating near record breaking heat for a few days next week with \nhighs in the 90s each afternoon. These temperatures appear to be \nwell overdone as EC/Canadian ensembles are cooler and the AIGFS/AI \nEuro are also leaning that way. In fact, the GEFS mean for high \ntemperatures in the middle to later half of next week is 5 degrees \nor higher for temperatures compared to the EC/Canadian ensemble \nmeans. So all that is to say that the NBM blended temperatures, \nwhich are currently in the forecast, may be skewed slightly on the \nwarmer side due to the apparent warm bias from the GEFS. There is \nfairly good agreement that Monday will be a warm day, with highs in \nthe mid to upper 80s. Beyond Monday is where the guidance really \nbegins to diverge.\n\nWith the flow over the Upper Midwest becoming westerly through the \nnext work week, this should continue the potential for nearly daily \nchances for showers and thunderstorms. There is some concern that \nthere may be chances for heavy rain in the middle of next week that \ncould lead to some flooding concerns, but due to the variance in the \nupper pattern, confidence in location, timing, etc. is low at this \ntime.",
